# cs429Prog11TinkerCache
Tinker simulator connected to cache implementation between simulator and memory.

Name: Praseedha Maddipatla

EID: psm2357

To compile:./build.sh

To run:./build/hw11

To compare the LRU and Random replacement policies, I used a few targeted access patterns that stress the L1-D cache in different ways. In Test 5A, I used a small pattern (A, B, A, C) that repeatedly accesses two useful lines while occasionally introducing a third conflicting line in the same 2-way set. In Test 5B, I ran a 16 KB array scan over four passes, where the working set fits entirely in L1-D after the initial warmup. Finally, in Test 6, I constructed a thrashing pattern by cycling through three addresses (A, B, C) that all map to the same 2-way set. Across all tests, I measured L1-D miss counts as the main metric, since that is where the differences between LRU and Random show up most clearly.

The results show that neither policy is strictly better; the performance depends on the access pattern. In Test 5A, LRU outperformed Random (8001 misses vs. ~9612 on average) because it correctly keeps recently used lines (A and B) in the cache, while Random sometimes evicts them unnecessarily. In Test 5B, both policies performed identically (256 misses each) since the working set fits in the cache, so after the initial compulsory misses, replacement policy doesn’t matter. However, in the thrashing case (Test 6), Random clearly did better: LRU had a 100% miss rate (6000 misses), while Random averaged about 3992 misses (~67%). This happens because LRU is deterministic and always evicts the line that will be needed next in this adversarial pattern, while Random occasionally “gets lucky” and keeps a useful line. Overall, these results highlight that LRU works best when there is strong temporal locality, but can perform poorly in adversarial cases, whereas Random is less predictable but sometimes advantageous if thrashing.

Raw Test Results:

============================================
  TCache Test Suite
============================================

=== TEST 1: Basic read/write correctness ===
  Policy=LRU     errors=0  PASS
  Policy=RANDOM  errors=0  PASS

=== TEST 2: Cache line pointer lookups ===
  After read(0x0000): L1-D ptr=non-null  PASS=yes
  After read(0x0000): L2   ptr=non-null  PASS=yes
  Unloaded addr 0x20000: L1-D ptr=null  PASS=yes

=== TEST 3: INSTR vs DATA separation ===
  L1-I accesses=64 (expected 64)  PASS=yes
  L1-D accesses=64 (expected 64)  PASS=yes
  L1-I misses=1 (expected 1, one cold miss per 64-byte line)
  L1-D misses=1 (expected 1)

=== TEST 4: Write-back to main memory ===
  mem[0x0000] = 0xAB (expected 0xAB)  PASS=yes

=== TEST 5A: LRU vs Random – hot set with occasional spoiler ===
  Pattern: A, B, A, C on one 2-way set (16000 total accesses)
  LRU   L1-D misses=8001     L2 misses=3     
  RND   L1-D misses=9612     L2 misses=3       (avg over 20 seeds)
  Expected: LRU <= Random because recent lines are reused before the spoiler returns.

=== TEST 5B: LRU vs Random – working set fits in L1-D ===
  Scan: 16 KB x 4 passes
  LRU   L1-D misses=256      L2 misses=256   
  RND   L1-D misses=256      L2 misses=256     (avg over 20 seeds)
  Expected: both policies are similar once the working set fits after warmup.

=== TEST 6: LRU vs Random – thrashing pattern ===
  Pattern: A, B, C on one 2-way set (6000 total accesses)
  LRU   L1-D misses=6000     L2 misses=3     
  RND   L1-D misses=3992     L2 misses=3       (avg over 20 seeds)
  Expected: Random <= LRU because this pattern is adversarial to recency.

=== TEST 7: Modified (dirty) bit ===
  After read:  modified=0 (expected 0)  PASS=yes
  After write: modified=1 (expected 1)  PASS=yes

============================================
  All tests complete.
============================================